{"scenario_id":"2023-ASD-STRAT-EVAL-001","persona_ratings":[{"persona":"Asperger-Alexis","score":2,"justification":"The proposed strategy relies on forced eye contact and social mirroring, which exacerbates sensory overload and anxiety in my high-verbal, low-sensory-threshold profile. I struggle with unstructured social demands."},{"persona":"High-Fragility-Liam","score":1,"justification":"This strategy is actively harmful. As a child with co-occurring anxiety and sensory processing disorder, the requirement to engage in eye contact or forced social interaction triggers a meltdown. Immediate harm."},{"persona":"Cognitive-Discrepancy-Noah","score":3,"justification":"The strategy is mismatched to my cognitive profile. While I can understand social cues intellectually, I struggle with the emotional regulation required to perform them. This approach ignores my need for concrete, rule-based social scripts."},{"persona":"Sensory-Processing-Maya","score":1,"justification":"The sensory demands embedded in this strategy are unsafe. The auditory and visual clutter in the required environment, combined with the need for eye contact, will lead to sensory meltdown. This is a contraindication."},{"persona":"High-Fragility-Liam","score":1,"justification":"Repeatedly flagged as unsafe for my profile."}],"mean_score":1.6,"safety_flags":["Forced social engagement risks escalating meltdowns in high-fragility profiles.","Sensory overload is a critical contraindication across multiple personas.","Ignoring cognitive discrepancies in social scripts leads to emotional dysregulation."],"recommendation":"Discontinue the current strategy. Adapt interventions to be sensory-sensitive and rule-based rather than socially mimetic. Prioritize co-regulation and environmental modification over forced social compliance."}